---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import GuideIndex from '../components/GuideIndex.astro';
import DownloadPanel from '../components/DownloadPanel.astro';
import { TERMINAL_GUIDE_URL, LIFECYCLE_GUIDE_URL } from '../consts.js';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
const toc = [["mac", "Get the Mac app"], ["first-project", "Your first project"], ["terminal", "Terminal setup"], ["stop", "Stop and return"], ["backup", "Backups and reset"]];
---
<Base title="Install Embodent" description="Get Embodent for Mac with Apple Silicon and set up your first agent project.">
<div class="wrap guide-header">
<h1>Make room for your AI team.</h1>
<p class="intro">Install Embodent, choose a project folder and bring in your agents.</p>
<details class="toc-mobile">
<summary>On this page</summary>
<GuideIndex links={toc} />
</details>
</div>
<div class="wrap guide-grid">
<aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
<strong>Installation</strong>
<GuideIndex links={toc} />
</aside>
<article class="guide-content">
<section class="guide-section" id="mac">
<h2>Get Embodent for Mac</h2>
<p>For Mac with Apple Silicon (M1 or later). The app includes the local runtime; you don’t need Docker or Homebrew for this setup.</p>
<DownloadPanel />
<ol>
<li>
<strong>Open the download.</strong> Open the DMG and move Embodent to Applications.</li>
<li>
<strong>Open Embodent.</strong> Follow the setup prompts and choose your project folder.</li>
<li>
<strong>Connect your agents.</strong> Use an installed and signed-in Claude Code or Codex. Each provider’s access and billing terms apply.</li>
</ol>
<p>Need a coding agent first? See <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/setup">Claude Code setup</a> or <a href="https://developers.openai.com/codex/cli/">Codex CLI setup</a>.</p>
<div class="notice">
<strong>Your project runs locally.</strong> Agent requests still go to the AI provider you use.</div>
</section>
<section class="guide-section" id="first-project">
<h2>Your first project</h2>
<p>Choose a folder and describe the outcome you want. Create your agent team, then use a workflow to organize the work. Give each task an outcome, an assignee and the approval setting you want.</p>
<a class="text-link" href={`${base}usage#workflows`}>Read the workflow guide</a>
</section>
<span class="legacy-anchor" id="advanced" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<section class="guide-section" id="terminal">
<h2>Prefer the terminal?</h2>
<p>The command-line tool keeps its name: <code>orcha</code>. For the current terminal installation and upgrade steps on macOS, use the source guide.</p>
<a class="text-link" href={TERMINAL_GUIDE_URL}>Open the terminal installation guide</a>
<p class="small">The terminal guide installs the current development version from main; it is separate from the Mac release above. The older Homebrew package predates the current local runtime. Don’t use it for a new setup.</p>
</section>
<span class="legacy-anchor" id="uninstall" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<section class="guide-section" id="stop">
<h2>Stop and come back later</h2>
<p>In your project folder, stop the local service while keeping your project history:</p>
<div class="code-block">
<div class="code-label">
<span>Stop this project</span>
<button type="button" class="copy enhanced-only" data-copy="stop-code" aria-label="Copy stop command">Copy</button>
</div>
<pre id="stop-code">orcha down</pre>
<p class="copy-result" role="status" aria-live="polite">
</p>
</div>
<p>Start the same project again when you return:</p>
<div class="code-block">
<div class="code-label">
<span>Start this project</span>
<button type="button" class="copy enhanced-only" data-copy="start-code" aria-label="Copy start command">Copy</button>
</div>
<pre id="start-code">orcha up</pre>
<p class="copy-result" role="status" aria-live="polite">
</p>
</div>
<p class="small">A configured macOS login service can start the project again at your next login. Stopping a service is different from putting a workflow on hold.</p>
</section>
<span class="legacy-anchor" id="reset" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<section class="guide-section" id="backup">
<h2>Keep a backup before resetting</h2>
<p>Resetting a project can erase its tasks, agents and history. It is separate from ordinary stopping. Make a backup first and keep the backup files together.</p>
<div class="code-block">
<div class="code-label">
<span>Back up this project</span>
<button type="button" class="copy enhanced-only" data-copy="backup-code" aria-label="Copy backup command">Copy</button>
</div>
<pre id="backup-code">orcha backup</pre>
<p class="copy-result" role="status" aria-live="polite">
</p>
</div>
<details class="guide-detail">
<summary>Restoring or removing project data</summary>
<p>Restoring replaces the current project data with a saved backup. Stop the project before restoring, check the backup you selected, then start the project again. Use the command’s help for the supported options.</p>
<p>The delete-data flag is never part of the ordinary stop instructions above. Use it only when you intend to erase that project’s history.</p>
<a class="text-link" href={LIFECYCLE_GUIDE_URL}>Read the lifecycle and recovery instructions</a>
</details>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</Base>
